1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,725
You'd never expect rocks to move by themselves.

2
00:00:02,745 --> 00:00:04,807
But in Death Valley, they leave tracks up

3
00:00:04,827 --> 00:00:07,521
to 100 meters long. What moves them, actually?

4
00:00:07,541 --> 00:00:10,141
That's the first of six strange places on Earth.

5
00:00:10,161 --> 00:00:11,261
For each one, we'll look at what

6
00:00:11,281 --> 00:00:13,406
scientists actually found, and give an honest

7
00:00:13,426 --> 00:00:16,550
verdict: solved, best guess, or still unknown.

8
00:00:16,570 --> 00:00:18,502
Number one: the sailing stones.

9
00:00:18,522 --> 00:00:21,168
In Death Valley National Park in California, there's

10
00:00:21,188 --> 00:00:23,970
a dry lake bed called Racetrack Playa. It's

11
00:00:23,990 --> 00:00:26,955
flat, cracked, and almost perfectly level.

12
00:00:26,975 --> 00:00:29,662
Scattered across it are rocks. And behind many of

13
00:00:29,682 --> 00:00:33,073
them are long, shallow trails, as if the rocks

14
00:00:33,093 --> 00:00:35,131
had slid across the ground on their own.

15
00:00:35,151 --> 00:00:37,746
Some trails are straight. Some curve. Some rocks

16
00:00:37,766 --> 00:00:40,390
that start side by side travel together for

17
00:00:40,410 --> 00:00:42,630
a while, and then one turns away.

18
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:45,337
The first written account comes from 1915, when

19
00:00:45,357 --> 00:00:48,848
a prospector named Joseph Crook visited the playa.

20
00:00:48,868 --> 00:00:50,780
For almost a century after that, nobody

21
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:53,238
saw a rock move. People blamed strong

22
00:00:53,258 --> 00:00:56,248
winds, dust devils, and slippery mud.

23
00:00:56,268 --> 00:00:59,750
Then, in the winter of 2013 and 2014, a

24
00:00:59,770 --> 00:01:03,395
research team put GPS trackers inside some rocks and

25
00:01:03,415 --> 00:01:05,578
set up time-lapse cameras around the playa.

26
00:01:05,598 --> 00:01:07,123
And they got lucky. On the twentieth

27
00:01:07,143 --> 00:01:09,850
of December, 2013, they watched more than

28
00:01:09,870 --> 00:01:11,770
sixty rocks move at once.

29
00:01:11,790 --> 00:01:14,197
Here's what happens. Rain or snowmelt makes a

30
00:01:14,217 --> 00:01:16,580
shallow pond on the playa. On cold winter

31
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:18,913
nights, the water freezes into a sheet of

32
00:01:18,933 --> 00:01:21,253
ice just a few millimeters thick.

33
00:01:21,273 --> 00:01:23,086
On a sunny morning, the ice starts to

34
00:01:23,106 --> 00:01:26,540
break into large thin panels. Light winds push

35
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:28,955
the panels across the water, and the panels

36
00:01:28,975 --> 00:01:31,921
shove the rocks along the soft mud underneath.

37
00:01:31,941 --> 00:01:34,266
The rocks move slowly, up to about five

38
00:01:34,286 --> 00:01:36,493
meters a minute. Some moves lasted up to

39
00:01:36,513 --> 00:01:39,388
sixteen minutes, and some rocks moved more than

40
00:01:39,408 --> 00:01:40,921
two hundred meters that winter.

41
00:01:40,941 --> 00:01:43,165
It only happens when the playa floods and then

42
00:01:43,185 --> 00:01:46,845
freezes, so in many winters, nothing moves at all.

43
00:01:46,865 --> 00:01:50,123
Verdict: solved. Thin sheets of ice, pushed by wind.

44
00:01:50,143 --> 00:01:52,101
Number two: the eye of the Sahara.

45
00:01:52,121 --> 00:01:54,728
In the desert of Mauritania, in West Africa,

46
00:01:54,748 --> 00:01:57,149
there's a giant bull's-eye. It's called the Richat

47
00:01:57,169 --> 00:01:59,101
Structure, or the Eye of Africa.

48
00:01:59,121 --> 00:02:01,929
It's about 40 kilometers across. From the ground, it's

49
00:02:01,949 --> 00:02:05,451
hard to even notice. But from space, it's unmistakable.

50
00:02:05,471 --> 00:02:06,564
NASA says it became a

51
00:02:06,584 --> 00:02:09,328
landmark for space shuttle crews.

52
00:02:09,348 --> 00:02:11,623
Because it's so round, scientists first

53
00:02:11,643 --> 00:02:13,530
thought a meteorite had made it.

54
00:02:13,550 --> 00:02:14,767
But when they studied the rocks in the

55
00:02:14,787 --> 00:02:17,732
1950s and 60s, they found none of the

56
00:02:17,752 --> 00:02:20,846
signs a high-speed impact leaves behind.

57
00:02:20,866 --> 00:02:23,949
The real story is slower. Long ago, molten

58
00:02:23,969 --> 00:02:26,340
rock pushed up from below and bent the

59
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:28,010
layers above it into a dome.

60
00:02:28,030 --> 00:02:30,505
Over a very long time, wind and water

61
00:02:30,525 --> 00:02:32,037
wore the top of that dome away. The

62
00:02:32,057 --> 00:02:34,944
harder rock layers survived as rings, and the

63
00:02:34,964 --> 00:02:37,901
softer ones eroded into valleys between them.

64
00:02:37,921 --> 00:02:40,021
That's why the oldest rocks are in the center,

65
00:02:40,041 --> 00:02:42,354
and the rings get younger as you move outward.

66
00:02:42,374 --> 00:02:44,949
The area is also full of ancient stone

67
00:02:44,969 --> 00:02:47,619
tools, left by early humans long before the

68
00:02:47,639 --> 00:02:49,039
desert was what it is today.

69
00:02:49,059 --> 00:02:51,222
Online, some people claim it's the lost

70
00:02:51,242 --> 00:02:54,611
city of Atlantis, because Plato described Atlantis

71
00:02:54,631 --> 00:02:56,181
as a city of rings.

72
00:02:56,201 --> 00:02:59,038
But there's no archaeological evidence of a city there,

73
00:02:59,058 --> 00:03:01,595
and no sign of the canals Plato described. Most

74
00:03:01,615 --> 00:03:04,890
scholars think Atlantis was a story Plato invented.

75
00:03:04,910 --> 00:03:07,237
Verdict: solved. An eroded rock dome,

76
00:03:07,257 --> 00:03:09,252
not a crater, and not Atlantis.

77
00:03:09,272 --> 00:03:10,930
Number three: the pink lake.

78
00:03:10,950 --> 00:03:12,800
On Middle Island, off the south coast of

79
00:03:12,820 --> 00:03:15,515
Western Australia, there's a lake that's bright pink.

80
00:03:15,535 --> 00:03:18,705
It's called Lake Hillier. It's about 600 meters long,

81
00:03:18,725 --> 00:03:21,468
and only a thin strip of sand and forest

82
00:03:21,488 --> 00:03:23,263
separates it from the dark blue ocean.

83
00:03:23,283 --> 00:03:25,159
The pink doesn't come from the light. If you

84
00:03:25,179 --> 00:03:27,861
scoop the water into a bottle, it stays pink.

85
00:03:27,881 --> 00:03:29,774
The explorer Matthew Flinders wrote

86
00:03:29,794 --> 00:03:32,052
about the lake in 1802.

87
00:03:32,072 --> 00:03:34,017
So where does the color come from? Life.

88
00:03:34,037 --> 00:03:36,974
The water is extremely salty. And very few

89
00:03:36,994 --> 00:03:38,557
things can live in it. One that can

90
00:03:38,577 --> 00:03:41,984
is a tiny alga called Dunaliella salina. Scientists

91
00:03:42,004 --> 00:03:44,625
believe it's the main source of the pink.

92
00:03:44,645 --> 00:03:46,870
A genetic study of the lake also found

93
00:03:46,890 --> 00:03:51,306
salt-loving bacteria, like Salinibacter ruber, and other microbes.

94
00:03:51,326 --> 00:03:54,272
In recent years, heavy rain has diluted the lake

95
00:03:54,292 --> 00:03:57,449
and faded its color. Scientists expect the pink to

96
00:03:57,469 --> 00:03:59,701
come back as the water level drops.

97
00:03:59,721 --> 00:04:03,644
Verdict: solved. Salt-loving microbes paint the lake pink.

98
00:04:03,664 --> 00:04:05,435
Number four: the door to hell.

99
00:04:05,455 --> 00:04:07,255
In the middle of the Karakum Desert

100
00:04:07,275 --> 00:04:09,200
in Turkmenistan, there's a crater that has

101
00:04:09,220 --> 00:04:11,082
been on fire for decades.

102
00:04:11,102 --> 00:04:13,682
It's about 60 to 70 meters wide and

103
00:04:13,702 --> 00:04:16,590
30 meters deep. Hundreds of natural gas fires

104
00:04:16,610 --> 00:04:18,980
burn across its floor and rim.

105
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,113
It's called the Darvaza gas crater, or

106
00:04:21,133 --> 00:04:22,721
simply the door to hell. It became

107
00:04:22,741 --> 00:04:24,954
the country's top tourist attraction.

108
00:04:24,974 --> 00:04:26,836
The most common story goes like this. In

109
00:04:26,856 --> 00:04:30,356
1971, Soviet engineers were drilling for oil and

110
00:04:30,376 --> 00:04:33,150
gas. The drill hit a cavern of natural

111
00:04:33,170 --> 00:04:35,203
gas, and the ground collapsed.

112
00:04:35,223 --> 00:04:37,598
To stop poisonous gas from spreading, they set

113
00:04:37,618 --> 00:04:39,705
it on fire, expecting it to burn out

114
00:04:39,725 --> 00:04:41,434
in a few days. It didn't.

115
00:04:41,454 --> 00:04:43,117
But here's the problem. The records from that

116
00:04:43,137 --> 00:04:46,100
time are missing, classified, or out of reach.

117
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:48,625
Some local geologists say the crater actually

118
00:04:48,645 --> 00:04:50,881
formed in the 1960s, and was only

119
00:04:50,901 --> 00:04:52,926
set on fire in the 1980s.

120
00:04:52,946 --> 00:04:57,066
In 2022, Turkmenistan's president announced plans to put the

121
00:04:57,086 --> 00:05:00,444
fire out. By 2025, officials said the fire had

122
00:05:00,464 --> 00:05:02,964
shrunk to a third of its size, after new

123
00:05:02,984 --> 00:05:05,728
wells were drilled around it to capture the gas.

124
00:05:05,748 --> 00:05:09,093
Verdict: best guess. Drilling and a gas fire, but

125
00:05:09,113 --> 00:05:12,083
nobody is sure exactly when or how it started.

126
00:05:12,103 --> 00:05:14,236
Number five: the fairy circles.

127
00:05:14,256 --> 00:05:16,481
In the Namib Desert in southern Africa, the

128
00:05:16,501 --> 00:05:19,347
grass is covered in bare, round patches, each

129
00:05:19,367 --> 00:05:21,630
surrounded by a ring of taller grass.

130
00:05:21,650 --> 00:05:24,055
They appear across a band of desert about

131
00:05:24,075 --> 00:05:28,056
2,400 kilometers long. They're called fairy circles.

132
00:05:28,076 --> 00:05:31,415
In 2014, similar circles were also found

133
00:05:31,435 --> 00:05:33,892
in the Pilbara region of Western Australia.

134
00:05:33,912 --> 00:05:36,437
Scientists have argued about them for decades,

135
00:05:36,457 --> 00:05:38,119
and there are two main camps.

136
00:05:38,139 --> 00:05:41,483
The first camp blames termites. In 2013, a

137
00:05:41,503 --> 00:05:45,233
researcher named Norbert Juergens found sand termites in

138
00:05:45,253 --> 00:05:48,218
almost every circle he studied. The idea is

139
00:05:48,238 --> 00:05:50,133
that they eat the grass roots, and the

140
00:05:50,153 --> 00:05:52,441
bare patch traps rainwater underground.

141
00:05:52,461 --> 00:05:54,686
The second camp says the grass does it

142
00:05:54,706 --> 00:05:57,812
to itself. In a very dry place, plants

143
00:05:57,832 --> 00:06:00,951
compete for water, and that competition can naturally

144
00:06:00,971 --> 00:06:03,108
space them out into a regular pattern.

145
00:06:03,128 --> 00:06:05,774
In 2022, one team dug up hundreds of

146
00:06:05,794 --> 00:06:08,694
grasses inside and around the circles. They found

147
00:06:08,714 --> 00:06:10,614
the grass had died from lack of water,

148
00:06:10,634 --> 00:06:12,816
not from termites eating the roots.

149
00:06:12,836 --> 00:06:16,436
And in Australia, the Aboriginal Martu people have long

150
00:06:16,456 --> 00:06:19,956
linked the circles to harvester termites, and a 2023

151
00:06:19,976 --> 00:06:22,251
study with them supported that idea there.

152
00:06:22,271 --> 00:06:26,863
Verdict: still unknown. Termites, thirsty grass, or maybe both.

153
00:06:26,883 --> 00:06:29,165
Number six: the lights of Hessdalen.

154
00:06:29,185 --> 00:06:32,060
In a quiet valley in central Norway, people have

155
00:06:32,080 --> 00:06:35,512
reported strange lights since at least the 1930s.

156
00:06:35,532 --> 00:06:38,572
The Hessdalen lights are usually white, yellow, or red.

157
00:06:38,592 --> 00:06:41,125
They can float slowly, hover in the air, or

158
00:06:41,145 --> 00:06:43,976
race across the valley. Some last a few seconds.

159
00:06:43,996 --> 00:06:45,409
Some last more than an hour.

160
00:06:45,429 --> 00:06:48,478
Between late 1981 and mid-1984, they were

161
00:06:48,498 --> 00:06:50,673
seen fifteen to twenty times a week,

162
00:06:50,693 --> 00:06:52,119
and tourists came to watch.

163
00:06:52,139 --> 00:06:56,922
In 1983, volunteers started Project Hessdalen to study them.

164
00:06:56,942 --> 00:07:00,460
And in 1998, an automatic measuring station was set

165
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:02,863
up in the valley to record the lights.

166
00:07:02,883 --> 00:07:05,808
Some sightings turned out to be stars, planes, car

167
00:07:05,828 --> 00:07:08,559
headlights, or mirages. But not all of them.

168
00:07:08,579 --> 00:07:11,103
There are several ideas. One says dust from the

169
00:07:11,123 --> 00:07:14,010
area's rocks burns in the air in an unusual

170
00:07:14,030 --> 00:07:17,462
way. Another says radioactive radon gas from the ground

171
00:07:17,482 --> 00:07:20,939
could turn dusty air into glowing plasma. Another points

172
00:07:20,959 --> 00:07:23,584
to electric charges from quartz in the rocks.

173
00:07:23,604 --> 00:07:24,866
None of these has been proven.

174
00:07:24,886 --> 00:07:27,032
Verdict: still unknown. The lights are

175
00:07:27,052 --> 00:07:28,735
real. What makes them isn't clear.

176
00:07:28,755 --> 00:07:30,313
So, let's check the scoreboard.

177
00:07:30,333 --> 00:07:33,037
The sailing stones: solved. Ice and wind.

178
00:07:33,057 --> 00:07:35,697
The eye of the Sahara: solved. An eroded dome.

179
00:07:35,717 --> 00:07:38,531
The pink lake: solved. Salt-loving microbes.

180
00:07:38,551 --> 00:07:40,321
The door to hell: best guess. A

181
00:07:40,341 --> 00:07:42,499
drilling accident and a gas fire.

182
00:07:42,519 --> 00:07:44,896
The fairy circles: still unknown.

183
00:07:44,916 --> 00:07:47,320
The Hessdalen lights: still unknown.

184
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:49,646
Three solved, one best guess, and two

185
00:07:49,666 --> 00:07:51,699
we still can't explain. Our own planet

186
00:07:51,719 --> 00:07:54,031
still has places that keep scientists guessing.

187
00:07:54,051 --> 00:07:56,149
Photos are credited on screen. The 3D

188
00:07:56,169 --> 00:07:58,869
scenes are illustrations based on the sources.

189
00:07:58,889 --> 00:08:00,514
Which place surprised you the most?

190
00:08:00,534 --> 00:08:01,708
Tell me in the comments.
